\details{
The Hill number of order \eqn{q} is defined as:
\deqn{{}^qD = \left(\sum_{i = 1}^{n} P_i^q\right)^{1/(1-q)}}

with its limits \eqn{{}^1D = e^H} (\eqn{H}: Shannon's entropy) and
\eqn{{}^\infty D = 1 / \max(P_i)}.

Where:
\itemize{
\item \eqn{n} : The number of features.
\item \eqn{P_i} : Proportional abundance of the \eqn{i}-th feature.
}

The order sets how much weight abundant features get. The Hill numbers
are a profile that unifies the common indices:
\itemize{
\item \eqn{q = 0}: richness, \code{observed()}.
\item \eqn{q = 1}: the exponential of Shannon's entropy, \code{exp(shannon())},
which weighs every feature by its frequency (Jost 2006).
\item \eqn{q = 2}: the inverse Simpson index, \code{inv_simpson()}.
\item \eqn{q = \infty}: the inverse Berger-Parker index, \code{1 / berger()}.
}

The default order, 2, is scikit-bio's, which makes \code{hill()} equal to
\code{inv_simpson()}; give \code{q} for any other order.
\code{\link[=renyi]{renyi()}} is \code{log(hill())}, and \code{\link[=tsallis]{tsallis()}} is another function of it.

Observed Hill numbers grow with sequencing depth, most for small
\eqn{q}; compare samples at equal depth, for instance with
\code{\link[=alpha_div_rarefied]{alpha_div_rarefied()}}.

